49. Market Structure, Concentration and Monopoly Power
Paper: Industrial Economics (ECNDSE303) | Unit: I | Topic: Introduction to industrial economics; firm, industry, market structure, market power, conduct and performance, market concentration and monopoly power
Introduction
Industrial economics studies the structure, conduct and performance of firms and industries, and the role of public policy toward them. It examines how firms compete, price, invest and grow, and what this means for efficiency and welfare.
Scope and significance
It covers firm organisation, market structure, pricing, mergers, innovation, location, finance and industrial policy. Significance: industry drives growth, employment and exports; understanding competition guides regulation and development policy.
Basic concepts
• Firm: a productive unit that organises inputs to produce output (Coase: it exists to economise on transaction costs).
• Industry: a group of firms producing close substitutes.
• Market: the arena in which buyers and sellers interact for a product, defined by product and geography.
• Market structure: number and size of firms, product differentiation, entry barriers, cost structure: perfect competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, monopoly.
• Market power: ability to raise price above marginal cost profitably, measured by the Lerner index L = (P - MC)/P = 1/|e|.
• Conduct: pricing, advertising, R&D, collusion, mergers.
• Performance: efficiency, profitability, innovation, equity.
The Structure-Conduct-Performance paradigm
Mason and Bain: structure determines conduct, which determines performance. High concentration and entry barriers lead to collusion and high profits. Critique: Chicago school (Demsetz, Stigler) argues efficiency explains concentration; contestability theory (Baumol) shows that potential entry disciplines even monopolies; modern game theory views structure as endogenous.
Market concentration
Measures:
• Concentration ratio CR_n: share of top n firms (CR4, CR8).
• Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI): sum of squared market shares (0 to 10,000); above 2,500 is considered highly concentrated in US guidelines.
• Gini coefficient and Lorenz curve of firm sizes; entropy index.
Monopoly power
Sources: economies of scale, patents, control of inputs, network effects, government licences, predatory behaviour. Costs: deadweight loss (Harberger triangle), rent-seeking (Tullock), X-inefficiency (Leibenstein), reduced innovation. Possible benefits: scale economies and R&D (Schumpeter). Measures of monopoly power: Lerner index, Bain index, price-cost margin. Control: antitrust (Competition Act 2002 in India, CCI), regulation, public ownership.
Conclusion
Market structure shapes behaviour and welfare, but causality runs both ways. Competition policy should target abuse of market power rather than size alone.